# Next.js page structure

A `page.tsx` file exports **one** route component: the default `Page` (plus optional `metadata` / `generateMetadata` / route config). Do not define sibling async components in the same file.

## Rules

- Put data fetching, `searchParams` / `params` awaiting, and JSX in the default `Page` itself.
- Prefer `export default async function Page(...)` when the route needs async work.
- Never add helpers like `PlayersGrid`, `PageContent`, or `AsyncSection` inside `page.tsx`.
- Do not extract page fetches into sibling component files just to satisfy Suspense — keep the fetch in `Page` and wrap the rendered UI in `<Suspense>` when needed.
- Layout chrome, loading UI, and client wrappers stay in `layout.tsx`, `loading.tsx`, or `components/`.

## Examples

```tsx
// ❌ BAD — nested async component in the page file
async function PlayersGrid({ searchParams }: Props) {
  const data = await fetchRoster(searchParams);
  return <Grid players={data} />;
}

export default function Page({ searchParams }: Props) {
  return (
    <Suspense fallback={<GridLoading />}>
      <PlayersGrid searchParams={searchParams} />
    </Suspense>
  );
}
```

```tsx
// ✅ GOOD — fetch in Page, Suspense around the UI
export default async function Page({ searchParams }: Props) {
  const sp = await searchParams;
  const players = await listPublicRosterPlayers(/* ... */);

  return (
    <HomeRoster>
      <Suspense fallback={<GridLoading />}>
        <Grid players={players} />
      </Suspense>
    </HomeRoster>
  );
}
```
